        "text": "{\"scores\":{\"heroes\":2,\"craft\":3,\"facts\":4,\"age_fit\":3,\"continuity\":4,\"delight\":4},\"evidence\":{\"heroes\":\"Cruz and Phoenix mostly look out of the window while Grandpa Greenie explains. Their only actions are squeezing a nose and using the grabber arm to pick up a tooth ('Cruz used the submarine's LEGO grabber arm to pick it up'), so they never solve a problem. Their lines are interchangeable reactions ('Whoa,' said the boys together), and nothing distinguishes the 4-year-old from the 7-year-old.\",\"craft\":\"Strong frame: a real-world cold open with the LEGO shark, the brick clicking to launch the submarine, WHOOOOSH as a shout-along, and a keepsake tooth with a satisfying 'Older than dinosaurs' / 'Older than TREES' callback. But chapters 3-6 are a parade of exhibits with no mini-challenge and no escalating stakes, which is close to a fact list with a thin frame.\",\"facts\":\"Facts are largely accurate: Greenland shark longevity, cartilage, sharks older than trees, Megalodon teeth, 500+ species, whale shark spots, electroreception, tiger shark number plates, lanternshark glow, epaulette walking and conveyor-belt teeth. Stethacanthus is nicely hedged ('Scientists still aren't quite sure'). However, the facts are delivered almost entirely as Grandpa Greenie lectures. 'Smell a tiny drop of blood from very far away' is an unhedged popular exaggeration. Stethacanthus and Helicoprion share a scene despite being tens of millions of years apart, and Helicoprion is not strictly a shark.\",\"age_fit\":\"Sentences are short with plenty of sound words, and there are big-kid hooks such as Helicoprion, cartilage and K-Pg survival. But band A requires no peril, and the Megalodon hush ('All the little fish had swum away to hide... A shadow slid over the submarine'), the great white's 'Rows and rows of teeth!' with Phoenix gripping Cruz's hand, and the mention of blood all add fear beats. At 1851 words the story is over the 1700 target, though within the ±15% tolerance.\",\"continuity\":\"The red brick is reused correctly: it clicks and glows to start the adventure and brings them home from the control panel. WHOOOOSH is used generously, and the opening recalls the LEGO-history trip. It does not reference the previous ending (the tower brick that went CLICK), and 'Play well' is swapped for 'Swim well'. Grandpa Greenie is a single, charming new recurring guide.\",\"delight\":\"There are jokes that land for both parent and child ('The Tooth Fairy must be SO busy', 'a nightlight shark', a shark 'carrying a table'), a slow-thinking Greenland shark gag and a warm goodnight. Some stretches read as flat lecture, notably the conservation paragraph and the 'Sharks have tried out lots of shapes' summary.\"},\"caps_applied\":[\"none\"],\"disqualified\":false,\"overall\":3.25,\"editor_notes\":[\"Give Cruz and Phoenix something to do in every chapter. For example, Phoenix could spot the walking shark's route between rock pools, Cruz could work out why the whale shark is harmless, and one of them could figure out that the little fish are hiding. Let the older child make a decision or ask the 'big kid' question in each chapter.\",\"Remove the fear beats for band A: drop the silent, everyone-hiding build-up to Megalodon, the great white's 'Rows and rows of teeth!' and hand-holding, and the drop-of-blood line. Replace them with wonder or silliness, which also trims the story back towards 1700 words.\",\"Open with a callback to the last story's tower brick that went CLICK and keep the 'Play well' catchphrase. Also turn the Grandpa Greenie monologues, especially the conservation speech, into back-and-forth exchanges where the children discover the facts themselves.\"],\"best_moment\":\"\\\"The Tooth Fairy must be SO busy,\\\" said Phoenix. Grandpa Greenie laughed so hard that bubbles came out of his gills.\",\"worst_moment\":\"This time, the water went very, very quiet. All the little fish had swum away to hide.\"}"
